<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumul Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kumul uprising</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a> Rebellion was an uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and other Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> that began at Hami, or Kumul, in 1931 after the provincial governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-shuren">Jin Shuren</a> abolished the Kumul Khanate. The rebels called in the Hui warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-zhongying">Ma Zhongying</a>, and the revolt spread across the province, helping to spark the short-lived First East Turkestan Republic in 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumul-rebellion.jpg" alt="Kumul Rebellion" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, photographed in the Republic of China · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkic_Conscripts_36th_division_1933_Kumul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
